---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import StageTrack from "../components/StageTrack.astro";
import { brandAsset } from "../lib/brand";
import { data } from "../lib/export";
import { SITE } from "../../site.config.mjs";
import { STAGE_PATH, STATUS_LABELS, STATUS_MEANINGS, overlayText } from "../lib/status";

const codes = Object.keys(STATUS_LABELS) as (keyof typeof STATUS_LABELS)[];
---

<Base
  title="How it works"
  description="How a claim's status is set from approved records, what each status means, and what this site does not say."
>
  <h1 class="title">How it works</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Each page tracks one reported breakthrough and shows the records found since it was reported.
    This page explains how the status on a claim page is set and what it does not tell you.
  </p>

  {/*
    The explainer, made in video/. It never plays on its own and nothing of it is fetched
    until the reader presses play. Its records are dated, because a video cannot be
    rebuilt with the site; the pages under it are the current word.
  */}
  <figure class="explainer">
    <video
      controls
      preload="none"
      playsinline
      width="1920"
      height="1080"
      poster={brandAsset("/video/explainer-poster.jpg")}
      aria-describedby="explainer-note"
    >
      {/* Raise the number with each new render, so a returning reader is not shown the old one. */}
      <source src="/video/explainer.mp4?v=2" type="video/mp4" />
      <a href="/video/explainer.mp4">Download the video</a>
    </video>
    <figcaption id="explainer-note">
      A video of 1 minute 52 seconds, with sound and captions, on how this site works. The records
      in it are as the registries showed them in November 2026; the claim pages are kept current.
    </figcaption>
    <details>
      <summary>What the video says</summary>
      <p>
        “New drug to regenerate lost teeth” (Kyoto University, 2021). “Multiplying teeth” (RIKEN,
        2015). “Regrowing Teeth” (Technische Universität Berlin, 2019). Headlines like these travel
        fast. The follow-up rarely does. So, years later: what came of it?
      </p>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} is a public record of what followed reported medical breakthroughs. Each claim
        gets one page: one treatment, for one condition. At the top is where it stands now. The
        video shows one claim, first reported in 2021, as it stood in November 2026.
      </p>
      <p>
        Underneath are the dated records that say so. Every record links to the registry that holds
        it, so you can check it yourself. And what was promised sits beside what was recorded.
      </p>
      <p>
        The status is set by fixed rules, from public registry records. No editor decides it, and no
        AI model does either. When nothing new has been recorded for years, the page says exactly
        that. That describes the records. It is not a verdict on the treatment.
      </p>
      <p>
        Seen a headline shared again? Paste the article's link into the box on the home page. If the
        claim is tracked, the answer comes back at once. The link is compared inside your browser,
        and sent nowhere.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is also a browser extension, for Chrome and Safari. Open it on an article, and it
        shows the tracked claim's status right there. If the article is not tracked, it can search
        the trial registries live. Those results are marked automatic and unreviewed, and every
        match shows its confidence score.
      </p>
      <p>No medical advice. No verdicts. Only what the records show, and when.</p>
    </details>
  </figure>

  {/* A long page on a phone: its sections, one tap away. */}
  <nav class="contents" aria-label="On this page">
    <ul>
      <li><a href="#how-a-status-is-set">How a status is set</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-each-status-means">What each status means</a></li>
      <li><a href="#no-signal">No signal</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-this-site-never-says">What this site never says</a></li>
      <li><a href="#where-the-data-comes-from">Where the data comes from</a></li>
      <li><a href="#an-optional-search-of-a-new-report">An optional search of a new report</a></li>
      <li><a href="#ways-to-keep-up">Ways to keep up</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-is-counted">What is counted</a></li>
      {SITE.correctionsUrl && <li><a href="#report-an-error">Report an error</a></li>}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 class="bar" id="how-a-status-is-set">How a status is set</h2>
  <p>
    A status is derived by fixed rules from the approved evidence events listed on the claim page.
    Where no trial is found, the rules also use the kind of study the first reports described.
    No model and no editor decides a status. Every status lists the events that produced it.
  </p>
  <p>The rules are applied in this order, and the first one that fits sets the status.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      If the paper the first news reports were about has a retraction notice, the status is
      “{STATUS_LABELS.retracted}”.
    </li>
    <li>If a regulator has recorded an approval, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.approved}”.</li>
    <li>
      Otherwise, trials that have not been terminated, withdrawn or marked as missing their primary
      endpoint are the ones that count. If any remain, the status follows the furthest one that has
      started: “{STATUS_LABELS.phase_3}”, “{STATUS_LABELS.phase_2}” or “{STATUS_LABELS.phase_1}”.
      If none has started, or none has a phase, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.trial_registered}”.
    </li>
    <li>
      If no trial remains and a person has marked a trial as missing its primary endpoint, with a
      source link, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.failed}”.
    </li>
    <li>
      If no trial remains and every registered trial was terminated or withdrawn, the status is
      “{STATUS_LABELS.terminated}”.
    </li>
    <li>
      With no trial found at all, the status depends on what the first reports described. If it was
      a study in people, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.no_trial_found}”. If it was laboratory or
      animal work, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.preclinical_only}”.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A claim that is still at a stage open to further evidence (“{STATUS_LABELS.preclinical_only}”
    through “{STATUS_LABELS.phase_3}”, and “{STATUS_LABELS.no_trial_found}”) can also carry the
    no-signal note, described below.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="what-each-status-means">What each status means</h2>
  <dl class="meanings">
    {
      codes.map((code) => (
        <div>
          <dt>
            {STAGE_PATH.includes(code) && <StageTrack code={code} />}
            {STATUS_LABELS[code]}
          </dt>
          <dd>{STATUS_MEANINGS[code]}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <h2 class="bar" id="no-signal">No signal</h2>
  <p>
    A claim that has stayed at an open stage for several years, with no new approved record, shows a
    note such as “{overlayText(5)}”. The note means that the most recent record is that old.
    The count starts from the date of that record, or from the date the claim was first reported
    when no record has been found.
  </p>
  <p>
    It says nothing about work that has not been published or registered, and it is not a status.
    A new record removes it.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="what-this-site-never-says">What this site never says</h2>
  <p class="plain">
    This site gives no verdict on a claim, a researcher, a company or a news outlet. It gives no
    medical advice and recommends no treatment. A registered clinical trial is not a treatment
    option.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="where-the-data-comes-from">Where the data comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Every record on a claim page links to the source that holds it. Records are shown as the
    registries and databases gave them. The summaries and the wording of statuses are the site’s.
    News articles are linked and summarised; their text is not copied.
  </p>
  <ul>
    {
      data.sources.map((source) => (
        <li>
          <a href={source.url}>{source.name}</a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2 class="bar" id="an-optional-search-of-a-new-report">An optional search of a new report</h2>
  <p>
    When you ask for a registry search, OpenAI reads the report's text to identify its treatment
    and condition. We search ClinicalTrials.gov and linked research through Europe PMC, then
    use OpenAI to compare the trial records. This is a live search of those research sources,
    not a general search of the web. Its answer is marked as automatic and unreviewed; it cannot
    create a claim page or change a status. We do not store submitted article text or screenshots.
    Structured answers for independently public reports may be reused for 24 hours; answers
    based only on browser content stay in that browser.
    Read <a href="/privacy">what is sent and how the providers handle it</a> before searching.
  </p>
  <p>
    Related papers come from OpenAlex searches for the treatment and condition. ROR identifies
    organisations explicitly named in the report. These sections open when you need them.
    A paper result does not establish that a treatment works, and an organisation record does
    not verify involvement or endorsement. Neither provider receives the article text, page
    address or screenshot. Public paper and organisation metadata may be cached for seven days.
  </p>
  <p>
    Opening the browser extension checks the current page automatically. A tracked article shows
    its saved claim. After you enable automatic search, an untracked page starts a live registry
    search when the service is available. If its text is insufficient, a screenshot of visible
    content can be sent. Select words first to search only those words, without links or a screenshot.
    Each candidate's match confidence is the model's estimate that the record concerns the reported
    treatment and condition. It is not a verified probability or a rating of treatment effectiveness
    or trial quality. Pages are not searched in the background while the extension is closed.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="ways-to-keep-up">Ways to keep up</h2>
  <p>
    A status can change months or years after a headline. None of these needs an account, and
    none tells us who you are.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Follow a claim.</strong> Press "Follow" on its page. The claim is then listed on
      the home page, with a note when its status changes. The list is kept in your browser
      only.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Since your last visit.</strong> The home page notes the day you open it, in your
      browser only, and next time says what is dated since.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check again later.</strong> A claim page can save a date to your calendar: the
      promised date, six months, a year, or the anniversary of the first report. The file is
      made on your device.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Feeds.</strong> For a feed reader: <a href="/changes.xml">every status change</a>,
      and <a href="/anniversaries.xml">anniversaries of first reports</a>. Each claim page and
      each topic page links a feed of its own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>On your own page.</strong> Each claim page gives a sentence to copy, a card to
      post, and a short piece of code that shows the claim's current status on another site.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="bar" id="what-is-counted">What is counted</h2>
  <p class="plain">Cloudflare, which hosts this site, counts requests and errors to operate the service. We do not add analytics, set tracking cookies, or keep reader profiles. Request logging for our Worker is disabled.</p>

  {
    /* Said only where a claim page does carry the link. */
    SITE.correctionsUrl && (
      <>
        <h2 class="bar" id="report-an-error">Report an error</h2>
        <p>Each claim page says how to report an error in it.</p>
      </>
    )
  }
</Base>
